cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PageLedger: Text from Scanned Documents, with a Record of How Each Page Was Read"
message: "If you use PageLedger in research, please cite the software, and separately cite the source documents and the PageLedger job or run behind the text you quote."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Busscher
    given-names: Peter
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0902-8195"
abstract: "PageLedger turns scanned and born-digital documents into text one page at a time, for historians, archivists and others who must say where a transcription came from. For every page it keeps, as plain files, which source file it came from, which OCR engine or model read it with which settings, what each reading said, why a page was held for a person to check, and who reviewed or corrected it. A record can be verified wherever it is deposited, so readers of an edition can trace any page back to its source. Verification shows that the record is intact; it does not certify that the text is right."
keywords:
  - OCR
  - transcription
  - digital humanities
  - archives
  - provenance
  - historical documents
  - document extraction
license: MIT
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21340651
    description: "Concept DOI resolving to the latest archived version of PageLedger."
repository-code: "https://github.com/peterbussch/pageledger"
version: "0.9.0"
date-released: "2026-11-13"
